**What was built** (all world-frame y-up, centred on the origin, 0.45 m wide × 0.47 m deep × 0.90 m tall, seat top at 0.45 m):

- **Seat** (root) — a 35 mm slab with rounded plan corners and rounded top/bottom rims, slightly lighter oak, hand-built as stacked rounded-rectangle loops so the rim is genuinely rounded rather than a sharp box.
- **Four legs** — square-section posts (46 mm) with rounded vertical arêtes, tapered below the stretcher line to a 33 mm foot, each fixed into the seat underside with a small seated tenon.
- **Four stretchers** — turned-look 22 mm round rails, two side and front/rear cross rails, all braced into their legs at 0.18 m above the floor.
- **Two back posts** — 46 mm square posts raked back 3°, standing on the seat surface at the rear corners and rising to the full 0.90 m height.
- **Back panel** — a 0.336 × 0.32 × 22 mm board with rounded corners and rims, tilted with the posts and jointed into their inner faces (1 mm seated embed each side), its top level with the post tops.

**Verification** — `run_tests()` proves the envelope (grounded, ~0.90 × 0.45 × 0.47 m, centred), the 0.45 m seat height and 35 mm slab, exact contact of every leg into the seat, every stretcher into both of its legs, both back posts onto the seat, and the panel into both posts; plus the panel sitting above the seat, within the seat width/depth, and the posts leaning behind the seat's rear edge. No allowances were needed — every joint is a flush or 1–2 mm seated fit that reads as real joinery.

