The model is complete and compiles cleanly.

**What was built** — a 500 mL glass bottle with a knurled aluminium screw cap (`model.py`):

- **Bottle (root part)**: a genuinely hollow lathe shell — punted/flat base with a he heel radius, 65 mm cylindrical body (150 mm straight), ellipsoidal shoulder, 13 mm neck with a tamper-evident retaining bead, thickened lip ring with a real open mouth and a ~2.4 mm bore running down into a 30 mm-radius interior cavity. Glass material is a translucent green-tinted soda-lime.
- **Moulded lettering** (frosted relief, no paper label): a stroke-font "500 mL" wrapped around the lower body front, plus "GLASS" and a recycling-triangle mark on the base annulus — both embedded ~0.9 mm into the glass so they read as moulded, not floating decals. I rasterised the shipped lettering OBJ to verify the glyphs read left-to-right as "500 mL".
- **Screw cap (child part)**: a 33.2 mm × 18 mm cup with closed top, rounded rim and 28 vertical knurl flutes, brushed-aluminium material; its ceiling seats flush on the lip face so it is captured by the neck rather than floating.
- **Articulation**: `bottle_to_cap` revolute about the neck axis (0,1,0), limits −0.20 → 12.566 rad, effort 2 N·m. Tests prove the cap stays concentric and seated at mid-spin, that it closes the bottle at ~220 mm overall, and that part diameters/height ratios match the brief. The coincident cap-seat/skirt relationship is declared with an element-scoped `allow_overlap` plus `expect_contact`/`expect_overlap` proofs.

